<Base
  title="Honor in a society of chieftains"
  description="Iliad Books 2 and 3, on this page: a lying dream, Thersites in the assembly, Helen on the wall, and a duel Aphrodite will not let end the war."
>
  <LessonChrome slug="kings-and-spectators">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Honor in a society of chieftains</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Today we add rank and reputation to the foundation. Homer, <em>Iliad</em>,
        Books 2 and 3, are in the reader.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="learn" aria-labelledby="will-learn">
      <h2 id="will-learn">What you’ll learn</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Zeus begins to keep his promise by sending Agamemnon a dream that says Troy will fall that day.</li>
        <li>The army tries to go home. Odysseus turns it back, and he silences Thersites, who had repeated Achilles’ charge.</li>
        <li>From the wall, Priam blames the gods for the war, and Helen wishes she had died rather than come.</li>
        <li>Paris and Menelaus fight for Helen. Aphrodite carries Paris away, so the settlement fails.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="lesson-body">
      <p class="scene-today">Today we add rank and reputation to the foundation.</p>
      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>An army of chieftains</h3>
      <p>
        The history lesson set two worlds side by side: the palace kingdoms the
        poems remember, and the later audience of the poet. The date when the
        epics were written down is debated. What you can see in the text, without
        that date, is the kind of army this is. It is not one king’s household
        troops alone. It is many kings, each with his followers, brought into one
        assembly under Agamemnon. Honor here is not a private feeling. It is a
        place in that order, and a name the assembly can hear.
      </p>
      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>The nod has to start somewhere</h3>
      <p>
        Lesson 1 laid honor as the first stone: a prize between two men, and no one
        above them who could settle it. Today we stay on that stone. We watch the
        same honor said out loud, in an army, and looked at from a wall. Let’s begin
        from the promise in Lesson 1. Zeus told Thetis the Trojans would
        have the victory until Achilles was honoured. Book 2 is the first step. The
        army sleeps. Zeus does not. He sends a dream in the shape of Nestor, the
        old man Agamemnon trusts. The dream says the gods are no longer divided,
        and that Agamemnon shall take Troy if he arms at once. Agamemnon believes
        it. The poem tells you he is wrong. Zeus still has many hard fights in
        store. The dream is a way to start a battle without Achilles, so the Greeks
        will feel that he is gone.
      </p>
      <p>
        Butler’s names again: Jove is Zeus, Juno is Hera, Ulysses is Odysseus,
        Minerva is Athena, Venus is Aphrodite. Alexandrus is Paris.
      </p>

      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>A test the army takes seriously</h3>
      <p>
        Agamemnon tells the elders the dream, and then he tests the army with the
        opposite speech: Zeus has played him false, and they should sail home to
        Argos without glory. The elders are supposed to hold the men if they bolt.
        They do bolt. The host runs for the ships. The poem says they would have
        gone home after a fashion that was not fated, had Hera not sent Athena to Odysseus. He has not
        put a hand to his own ship. He takes the sceptre and talks the army back
        into the assembly, kings in one voice and common men in another.
      </p>
      <p>
        Learn the shape of it. The dream was false. The test was a pretence. The
        soldiers’ answer was real. They are tired of the war. It takes a goddess
        and Odysseus to put them back.
      </p>

      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>The same charge, from a man with no rank</h3>
      <p>
        One man keeps talking. Thersites is a common soldier. Homer describes him
        as ugly, bandy-legged, and a railer at anyone in authority, before he lets
        you hear the speech. The speech is Achilles’ charge from Book 1, said in
        public: the king’s tents are full, the army gives him the pick of every
        town, Achilles was robbed of his prize, and the men should sail home and
        leave Agamemnon to sit on his rewards. “Meeds,” in Butler, means rewards.
      </p>
      <p>
        Odysseus forbids him to chide princes when he has no following, then beats
        him with the golden sceptre until a weal rises on his back. Thersites sits
        down in tears. The people are sorry for him, and they laugh, and they say
        Odysseus never did them a better turn than stopping this man’s mouth. The
        host marches. After that comes the Catalogue of Ships, a list of the
        contingents. You may skim it. What it shows is the kind of army this is:
        many kings, named one by one, under Agamemnon.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hold both halves. The poem lets a common soldier say that the king’s greed
        is the army’s misery. It then refuses to let that soldier lead. The laugh
        puts the assembly back under the princes.
      </p>

      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>Helen looks at the war</h3>
      <p>
        Book 3 opens on Paris. He sees Menelaus and shrinks back. Hector shames him.
        Paris offers a duel: the winner takes Helen and the goods that came with
        her, and the war ends. Menelaus accepts. Both armies are glad. They think
        they will have rest.
      </p>
      <p>
        Iris finds Helen weaving a purple web of the battles fought for her sake,
        and tells her to come and see. Helen goes to the wall in tears. The old men
        at the gate say it is small wonder the armies have endured so much for a
        woman so lovely, and that, fair though she is, the Greeks should take her
        and go, or she will breed sorrow for them and their children. Priam will
        not have that said to her. He seats her beside him and says he lays no
        blame on her. The gods brought the war. She wishes she had chosen death
        rather than leave her home, her daughter, and the companions of her
        girlhood. Then she names Agamemnon. She looks for her brothers, Castor and
        Pollux, and does not see them. She does not know they are already buried in
        their own land.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the war from inside Troy, before the duel. The cause, as Priam
        states it, is the gods. The cost, as Helen states it, is the life she left.
      </p>

      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>A goddess takes the loser away</h3>
      <p>
        They swear. If Paris kills Menelaus, he keeps Helen and the Greeks sail. If
        Menelaus kills Paris, the Trojans give Helen back and pay a fine. Zeus does
        not grant the prayer. Priam will not watch his son fight, and goes into the
        city.
      </p>
      <p>
        Menelaus prays to punish a man who wronged his host. His spear tears Paris’s
        tunic; Paris swerves and lives. The sword breaks on the helmet. Menelaus
        drags him by the horsehair plume toward the Greek lines. Aphrodite breaks
        the chin-strap. The helmet comes away empty. She hides Paris in a cloud and
        puts him in his own bedroom, then fetches Helen from the wall. Helen knows
        the goddess, answers in anger, is afraid, and goes. She rebukes Paris.
        He says Athena helped Menelaus this time, and asks her to bed.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the field, Menelaus searches for a man who is no longer there. Agamemnon
        declares the victory Menelaus’s and demands Helen and the fine. The Greeks
        shout approval. A human settlement was in reach. A god removed the man who
        had to be handed over. That is why the war does not stop in Book 3.
      </p>
    </div>

