    <section class="reflection" aria-labelledby="reflect-title">
      <h2 id="reflect-title">A short reflection</h2>
      <p>
        One optional sentence, if you want it: Thersites says in public what
        Achilles said in the quarrel, and the assembly laughs him down. Where have
        you seen a true complaint lose because of who said it? The poem’s scene is
        already told. The sentence is yours, and it stays in this browser.
      </p>
      <label class="reflection-label" for="note-kings">Your note</label>
      <textarea id="note-kings" data-note="kings-and-spectators" rows="4"></textarea>
    </section>

    <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="takeaways-title">
      <h2 id="takeaways-title">Key takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The layer is still honor. What this week adds is the society that honor
          lives in: kings, followers, and an assembly.
        </li>
        <li>
          The prize from Lesson 2 is now a speech in public. Thersites says what
          Achilles said. Odysseus beats him with the sceptre, and the army both
          pities him and laughs.
        </li>
        <li>
          Helen on the wall, and Priam’s refusal to blame her, show the same war
          from the other side. The cause Priam names is the gods. The cost Helen
          names is the life she left.
        </li>
        <li>
          The failure of Lesson 1 is still here. A human oath could have ended the
          war. Aphrodite removes Paris. Nothing in these books stands above honor
          and the gods who handle it.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="summary">
        Honor was a quarrel between two men. It is now a quarrel an army can hear
        and a city can watch, and it still has no judge both sides will obey.
      </p>
    </section>

    <aside class="go-deeper">
      <h2>Go deeper, if you want</h2>
      <p>
        The object the syllabus names this week is the gold mask Schliemann called
        the Mask of Agamemnon, in the National Archaeological Museum, Athens. It
        comes from Grave Circle A at Mycenae, about 1550–1500 BC, centuries older
        than the Trojan War as later Greeks told it. The name is Schliemann’s. The
        gold is Mycenaean. You do not need a book, or the mask, to finish the lesson.
      </p>
    </aside>

